{"number":59,"title":"Canon patch (blocked on push scope): executor-routine fixes for #53–#57","body":"The canon changes for #53, #54, #55, #56 and #57 are complete and tested — but **this session cannot push them**. The git proxy returns `403` for `missingbulb/Claudinite`; the session&#39;s GitHub scope is `missingbulb/claudinitewebsite` alone. That&#39;s an organization egress policy, which the proxy README says to report rather than route around.

So the work is preserved here, to be applied from a session whose environment includes the canon repo.

**Local commit:** `40f316c` — *Stop the executor routine tripping over its own conformance checks* (8 files, +273 / −12)

**Verified before this was written:**
- full canon suite — `1148 pass / 0 fail`
- `node engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs` — 0 blocking (9 advisories, all pre-existing on `main`, unchanged by this diff)
- every new test confirmed to **fail against the unmodified source first**, then pass
- the new `conformance-workflow` rule run against this repo&#39;s real `ci.yml`: 1 finding before the fix, 0 after

## To apply

```sh
git checkout -b claude/executor-routine-fixes
git apply canon-executor-fixes.patch
```

## Patch


<code>git diff main..HEAD</code>

````diff
diff --git a/bootstrap.md b/bootstrap.md
index a7b651d..bd12284 100644
--- a/bootstrap.md
+++ b/bootstrap.md
@@ -217,6 +217,36 @@ node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs
   whose steps run the project&#39;s own tests, if any, then the world command above. The point is a
   deterministic place the whole-repo sweep runs at each change; keep it as small as the repo needs.

+**If that flow is a GitHub Actions workflow, it needs `workflow_dispatch:` — one line, and
+without it the repo&#39;s nightly maintenance PR can never land.** Both the branch push and the PR
+open ride the Action&#39;s `GITHUB_TOKEN`, and GitHub&#39;s recursion guard suppresses workflow runs for
+events that token authors — so a maintenance PR gets **no `pull_request` run at all**, however
+unfiltered the workflow is. `maintenance.delivery: auto-merge` then arms a queue that waits on a
+check which never appears, and baselining stops landing silently. The guard&#39;s one documented
+exception is `workflow_dispatch`, which is why baselining&#39;s `deliver()` starts the checks itself.
+Give the workflow both triggers, and **no `paths:` filter** (a filtered sweep is a gate that
+happens to be open — the `conformance-workflow` check flags both shapes):
+
+```yaml
+name: CI
+on:
+  pull_request:            # no paths: filter — the sweep must gate every PR
+  push:
+    branches: [main]
+  workflow_dispatch:       # lets baselining start this run on the maintenance PR
+jobs:
+  conformance:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
+      - uses: actions/setup-node@v5
+        with:
+          node-version: 22
+      # the project&#39;s own tests go here, if it has any
+      - name: Claudinite world-scope conformance sweep
+        run: node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs
+```
+
 Then run the world sweep once locally and clear what it surfaces. On a repo with existing code,
 **expect a backlog** — enforcement scope is whole-repo, and findings in code you never touched
 would otherwise fail every future run. Fix causes, or record a reasoned `accept` in
diff --git a/engine/checks/helpers/session-transcript.mjs b/engine/checks/helpers/session-transcript.mjs
index 983e006..b07a8b1 100644
--- a/engine/checks/helpers/session-transcript.mjs
+++ b/engine/checks/helpers/session-transcript.mjs
@@ -5,6 +5,23 @@
 // string message content (tool results arrive as content arrays, injected/meta
 // turns carry isMeta, subagent traffic carries isSidechain, and synthetic turns
 // — hook output, reminders, webhook activity — are tag-wrapped, starting with &#34;&lt;&#34;).
+//
+// AND a routine trigger is not an owner comment. An unattended run (the scheduler
+// labels a dispatch issue; CCR starts a session with a stored launcher prompt)
+// arrives as a `type: &#34;user&#34;` entry carrying PLAIN STRING content with the
+// harness&#39;s `[SCHEDULED TASK …]` wrapper already stripped — &#34;Execute the
+// Claudinite executor: …&#34; and nothing else. None of the filters above see it:
+// it is not meta, not a sidechain, not tag-wrapped. Treated as an owner comment
+// it made every unattended executor session fail comment-classification for not
+// &#34;replying&#34; to a prompt no human wrote.
+//
+// The discriminator is `origin.kind`, verified against a real transcript:
+//   { kind: &#39;human&#39; }                                         — the owner typed it
+//   { kind: &#39;task-notification&#39;, subkind: &#39;scheduled-trigger&#39; } — a routine fired
+//   { kind: &#39;task-notification&#39; }                              — a subagent finished
+// A transcript predating the field carries no `origin` at all, so ABSENT means
+// &#34;unknown, judge it by shape&#34; — the tag-wrapped filter below still applies —
+// while PRESENT and non-human is a positive &#34;not the owner&#34;.

 export function parseEntries(text) {
   const entries = [];
@@ -17,6 +34,7 @@ export function parseEntries(text) {

 function humanText(entry) {
   if (entry.type !== &#39;user&#39; || entry.isMeta || entry.isSidechain) return null;
+  if (entry.origin &amp;&amp; entry.origin.kind !== &#39;human&#39;) return null;
   const content = entry.message?.content;
   const text = typeof content === &#39;string&#39;
     ? content
diff --git a/engine/scheduler/executor.md b/engine/scheduler/executor.md
index 6a3281b..95f5828 100644
--- a/engine/scheduler/executor.md
+++ b/engine/scheduler/executor.md
@@ -19,9 +19,17 @@ goes through your GitHub tools.
 1. **Resolve and validate your dispatch — in code, before anything else.**

    ```bash
-   node /scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs 
+   node /scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs ; echo &#34;resolve-dispatch exit=$?&#34;
    ```

+   **Invoke it exactly like that — with the `echo`.** Every verdict below except
+   `0` is a non-zero exit, and most of them are the shell working correctly: `13` is the
+   ordinary first half of the CCR two-shot handshake, and `11`/`12` are the anti-duplicate
+   guard doing its job. Run bare, those surface as a failed command — red in the session, and
+   a reader (or a later triage pass) sees a broken tool where nothing is broken. The trailing
+   `echo` makes the command itself succeed and prints the verdict for you to branch on; the
+   code is still the interface, it is just no longer disguised as a fault.
+
    `` is the engine root — the **parent** of the directory this file sits in (this file
    is `/scheduler/executor.md`), so the same `` addresses every command below.
    `` is the word your launcher prompt
@@ -44,7 +52,7 @@ goes through your GitHub tools.
    | exit | verdict | what you do |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | `0` | valid dispatch, yours | Quote the printed `brief:` line in chat (see below), then go to step 2. The printed block is your brief: issue, label, task path, pack, task, slot, model, outcome ceiling, `executionTimeout`. |
-   | `13` | issue named, body needed | Fetch **the printed issue and only it** over MCP, save the raw response JSON **verbatim** to a file, and re-run with `--issue-json ` — the shell extracts body, labels, and title itself, and refuses a response for the wrong issue. Act on *that* run&#39;s exit code. |
+   | `13` | issue named, body needed — **normal, not an error** | Fetch **the printed issue and only it** over MCP, save the raw response JSON **verbatim** to a file, and re-run with `--issue-json ` — the shell extracts body, labels, and title itself, and refuses a response for the wrong issue. Act on *that* run&#39;s exit code. Every CCR-triggered session takes this path; say so plainly rather than reporting a failure. |
    | `10` | invalid dispatch | It never runs. Comment the printed `reason`, remove the ready label, add `needs-human`, end the session. |
    | `11` | not yours | Another scope&#39;s dispatch, or one another session has already claimed. **Stop**: change nothing, comment nothing, end the session. One exception to the silence, in your final message only: if the printed `labelScope` is `fleet` and your prompt named no scope, say plainly that this looks like the fleet routine missing the `fleet` word — nothing on GitHub records that, the scheduler re-arms the dispatch every hour, and it will decline forever until a human reads it here. |
    | `12` | no trigger at all | **Stop**: run nothing, change nothing, comment nothing. There is no fallback — do not list the queue, do not take the oldest, do not take *any*. Say plainly in your final message that no trigger reached the shell; that is a defect worth a human seeing. |
diff --git a/engine/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs b/engine/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs
index 82c5ecb..a8f14ef 100644
--- a/engine/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs
+++ b/engine/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs
@@ -44,7 +44,18 @@
 // itself, it tells the EXECUTOR to fetch it over MCP and hand the bytes back.
 //
 // EXIT CODES ARE THE INTERFACE (see EXIT below). The executor branches on the
-// number, so each verdict has its own code and its own documented next step:
+// number, so each verdict has its own code and its own documented next step.
+//
+// A NON-ZERO CODE HERE IS A VERDICT, NOT A FAULT. Only `1` and `2` report that
+// this shell went wrong; `11`, `12` and `13` are it working exactly as designed
+// — the anti-duplicate guard declining a dispatch, and the ordinary first half
+// of the CCR two-shot handshake. They are distinct codes rather than a `0` with
+// a status word because the executor MUST NOT be able to mistake &#34;fetch the body
+// and come back&#34; for &#34;you have a valid dispatch, go run it&#34;: conflating those is
+// how the duplicate-execution bug gets back in, and a shell that exits 0 on every
+// path invites exactly that misread. The cost is cosmetic — a bare invocation
+// renders red — and executor.md pays it with a trailing `echo &#34;exit=$?&#34;`, which
+// keeps the code readable without dressing a normal handshake up as a failure.
 //
 //   0  valid       — a legal dispatch for this session&#39;s scope; go claim it.
 //   10 invalid     — a forged or mangled dispatch. Comment the printed `reason`,
diff --git a/packs-tests/basics/conformance-workflow.test.mjs b/packs-tests/basics/conformance-workflow.test.mjs
index 9512eba..916804f 100644
--- a/packs-tests/basics/conformance-workflow.test.mjs
+++ b/packs-tests/basics/conformance-workflow.test.mjs
@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@
 import { test } from &#39;node:test&#39;;
 import assert from &#39;node:assert/strict&#39;;
-import rule, { onBlock, gatesEveryPull } from &#39;../../packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs&#39;;
+import rule, { onBlock, gatesEveryPull, isDispatchable } from &#39;../../packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs&#39;;

 const MOUNT = &#39;.claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs&#39;;
 const SWEEP_STEP = &#39;run: node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs&#39;;
@@ -8,17 +8,34 @@ const SWEEP_STEP = &#39;run: node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.m
 // A ctx over an in-memory file map — no git, no fixture tree.
 const ctx = (files) =&gt; ({ files: Object.keys(files), read: (f) =&gt; files[f] ?? null });

+// The shape that actually works: unfiltered AND dispatchable. Both halves are
+// load-bearing — without workflow_dispatch the GITHUB_TOKEN recursion guard
+// means this never runs on the maintenance PR at all.
 const unfiltered = `name: Checks
 on:
   pull_request:
   push:
     branches: [main]
+  workflow_dispatch:
 jobs:
   checks:
     steps:
       - ${SWEEP_STEP}
 `;

+// ClaudiniteWebsite&#39;s shape before the fix: unfiltered, sweeps, but baselining
+// cannot start it — so auto-merge waits on a check that never appears.
+const undispatchable = `name: CI
+on:
+  pull_request:
+  push:
+    branches: [main]
+jobs:
+  conformance:
+    steps:
+      - ${SWEEP_STEP}
+`;
+
 const pathFiltered = `name: test extension
 on:
   pull_request:
@@ -51,10 +68,40 @@ test(&#39;the rule is inert in a repo that is not a Claudinite member&#39;, () =&gt; {
   assert.deepEqual(rule.run(ctx({ &#39;.github/workflows/ci.yml&#39;: pathFiltered })), []);
 });

-test(&#39;an unfiltered conformance workflow satisfies the rule&#39;, () =&gt; {
+test(&#39;an unfiltered, dispatchable conformance workflow satisfies the rule&#39;, () =&gt; {
   assert.deepEqual(rule.run(ctx({ [MOUNT]: &#39;&#39;, &#39;.github/workflows/checks.yml&#39;: unfiltered })), []);
 });

+test(&#39;isDispatchable reads workflow_dispatch out of the on: block only&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  assert.equal(isDispatchable(unfiltered), true);
+  assert.equal(isDispatchable(undispatchable), false);
+  // A job STEP mentioning the string must not count as a trigger.
+  assert.equal(isDispatchable(&#39;name: x\non:\n  pull_request:\njobs:\n  j:\n    steps:\n      - run: echo workflow_dispatch:\n&#39;), false);
+});
+
+// The fourth shape (#565&#39;s consumer-side twin): the gate is unfiltered and real,
+// but the GITHUB_TOKEN recursion guard means it never fires on a bot-authored PR
+// and baselining has no way to start it. Same dead end as the path filter.
+test(&#39;an unfiltered sweep with no workflow_dispatch is flagged, naming the one-line fix&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const out = rule.run(ctx({ [MOUNT]: &#39;&#39;, &#39;.github/workflows/ci.yml&#39;: undispatchable }));
+  assert.equal(out.length, 1);
+  assert.match(out[0].what, /no `workflow_dispatch:`/);
+  assert.match(out[0].what, /ci\.yml/);
+  assert.match(out[0].fix, /workflow_dispatch:/);
+  // It must NOT misdirect to the path-filter remedy, which is not this repo&#39;s problem.
+  assert.doesNotMatch(out[0].what, /path filter/);
+});
+
+// A dispatchable gate elsewhere is still a gate — the undispatchable one is then
+// just another workflow, not a trap.
+test(&#39;a dispatchable gate wins even when an undispatchable sweeping workflow also exists&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  assert.deepEqual(rule.run(ctx({
+    [MOUNT]: &#39;&#39;,
+    &#39;.github/workflows/ci.yml&#39;: undispatchable,
+    &#39;.github/workflows/checks.yml&#39;: unfiltered,
+  })), []);
+});
+
 // The TLDR shape, and the reason this rule exists: the arm succeeds, no check
 // ever runs, the maintenance PR waits forever.
 test(&#39;a path-filtered sweep is flagged, naming the file&#39;, () =&gt; {
diff --git a/packs-tests/basics/pack.test.mjs b/packs-tests/basics/pack.test.mjs
index ad09e10..1a2b45e 100644
--- a/packs-tests/basics/pack.test.mjs
+++ b/packs-tests/basics/pack.test.mjs
@@ -139,6 +139,39 @@ test(&#39;task-lifecycle: silent on main itself&#39;, () =&gt; {
   } finally { cleanup(root); }
 });

+// The scheduler&#39;s maintenance branch is written by baselining&#39;s preprocessing,
+// and the dispatch issue that tracks the cycle is filed only after preprocessing
+// returns — so the number cannot exist at commit time. Flagging it asked the
+// executor to force-push over a shared PR branch to satisfy a check.
+test(&#39;task-lifecycle: exempts the scheduler-authored maintenance branch&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;f.txt&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; }, commitMsg: &#39;Claudinite maintenance&#39; });
+  try {
+    git(root, &#39;branch&#39;, &#39;-m&#39;, &#39;claudinite/maintenance-2026-08-01-z0nih5&#39;);
+    assert.equal(run(taskLifecycle, root).length, 0);
+  } finally { cleanup(root); }
+});
+
+// The exemption is the machine branch family, not &#34;anything under claudinite/&#34;.
+test(&#39;task-lifecycle: still flags an ordinary branch that merely starts with claudinite/&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;f.txt&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; }, commitMsg: &#39;no reference here&#39; });
+  try {
+    git(root, &#39;branch&#39;, &#39;-m&#39;, &#39;claudinite/my-hand-written-branch&#39;);
+    assert.equal(run(taskLifecycle, root).length, 1);
+  } finally { cleanup(root); }
+});
+
+// The old fix text told the agent to amend — on a branch whose commits are
+// already pushed, that is a force-push over shared history the git-github pack
+// forbids, so the advice had no legal way to be followed.
+test(&#39;task-lifecycle: the remedy scopes amending to unpushed commits&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;f.txt&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; }, commitMsg: &#39;no reference here&#39; });
+  try {
+    const [f] = run(taskLifecycle, root);
+    assert.match(f.fix, /amend .*if it is still unpushed/);
+    assert.match(f.fix, /otherwise add a new commit/);
+  } finally { cleanup(root); }
+});
+
 test(&#39;warning-suppression: flags a newly added suppression marker&#39;, () =&gt; {
   const bad = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;a.js&#39;: &#39;x();\n// eslint-disable-next-line no-undef\ny();\n&#39; } });
   try {
@@ -618,6 +651,54 @@ test(&#39;comment-classification: tool results, meta, and tag-wrapped turns are not
   } finally { cleanup(root); }
 });

+// A routine trigger is not an owner comment. An unattended run arrives as a
+// plain-string user turn with the harness&#39;s `[SCHEDULED TASK …]` wrapper already
+// stripped, so none of the shape filters above see it — every nightly executor
+// session was failing this rule for not &#34;replying&#34; to a prompt no human wrote.
+// `origin.kind` is the discriminator, verified against a real transcript.
+test(&#39;comment-classification: a scheduled-routine trigger is not an owner comment&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;a.md&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; } });
+  const routine = (text) =&gt; ({
+    type: &#39;user&#39;,
+    timestamp: &#39;2026-08-01T03:58:00Z&#39;,
+    origin: { kind: &#39;task-notification&#39;, subkind: &#39;scheduled-trigger&#39; },
+    message: { role: &#39;user&#39;, content: text },
+  });
+  try {
+    // The exact shape that fired: a launcher prompt, then an unclassified reply.
+    assert.equal(runWithTranscript(commentClassification, root, [
+      routine(&#39;Execute the Claudinite executor: .claudinite/shared/engine/scheduler/executor.md&#39;),
+      reply(&#39;Running the dispatch now.&#39;),
+    ]).length, 0);
+
+    // A subagent-completion notification is equally not an owner comment.
+    assert.equal(runWithTranscript(commentClassification, root, [
+      { type: &#39;user&#39;, origin: { kind: &#39;task-notification&#39; }, message: { role: &#39;user&#39;, content: &#39;agent finished&#39; } },
+      reply(&#39;Converging the issue.&#39;),
+    ]).length, 0);
+
+    // But a real typed message still is one — the filter must not swallow the rule.
+    assert.equal(runWithTranscript(commentClassification, root, [
+      routine(&#39;Execute the Claudinite executor: .claudinite/shared/engine/scheduler/executor.md&#39;),
+      reply(&#39;Running the dispatch now.&#39;),
+      { type: &#39;user&#39;, origin: { kind: &#39;human&#39; }, message: { role: &#39;user&#39;, content: &#39;lgtm&#39; } },
+      reply(&#39;Merging it.&#39;),
+    ]).length, 1);
+  } finally { cleanup(root); }
+});
+
+// Transcripts written before the field exists carry no `origin`; absent must
+// mean &#34;judge it by shape&#34;, not &#34;not human&#34;, or the rule goes silent everywhere.
+test(&#39;comment-classification: a turn with no origin field is still judged by shape&#39;, () =&gt; {
+  const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;a.md&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; } });
+  try {
+    assert.equal(runWithTranscript(commentClassification, root, [
+      owner(&#39;please add the widget&#39;),
+      reply(&#39;On it.&#39;),
+    ]).length, 1);
+  } finally { cleanup(root); }
+});
+
 test(&#39;comment-classification: silent without a transcript (CI) and on an empty conversation&#39;, () =&gt; {
   const root = makeRepo({ changed: { &#39;a.md&#39;: &#39;x\n&#39; } });
   try {
diff --git a/packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs b/packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs
index fe879ed..8863d06 100644
--- a/packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs
+++ b/packs/basics/conformance-workflow.mjs
@@ -9,9 +9,10 @@ import { finding } from &#39;../../engine/checks/helpers/findings.mjs&#39;;
 // works:
 //
 //   no pull_request workflow at all  → baselining merges directly (#588). Fine.
-//   an UNFILTERED conformance flow   → the sweep runs, auto-merge lands on green.
+//   an UNFILTERED, DISPATCHABLE flow → the sweep runs, auto-merge lands on green.
 //   a PATH-FILTERED flow             → the arm succeeds, and no check ever runs.
 //                                      The PR waits forever. THIS is the trap.
+//   an UNDISPATCHABLE flow           → same ending, different cause. See below.
 //
 // The third shape is invisible: the repo looks like it has CI, `hasPrCi` is
 // true, the arm reports success, and the PR simply never lands. TLDR sat in it —
@@ -20,6 +21,21 @@ import { finding } from &#39;../../engine/checks/helpers/findings.mjs&#39;;
 // matched nothing. The same filters also hid two blocking `claudinite-isolation`
 // findings on its `main` for as long as they had existed.
 //
+// THE FOURTH SHAPE — no `workflow_dispatch:` — reaches the identical dead end by
+// a route the path-filter check cannot see. Both the branch push and the PR open
+// ride the Action&#39;s GITHUB_TOKEN, and GitHub&#39;s recursion guard suppresses
+// workflow runs for events that token authors: the maintenance PR gets NO
+// pull_request run at all, however unfiltered the workflow is. The guard&#39;s one
+// documented exception is `workflow_dispatch`, which is why baselining&#39;s
+// deliver() starts the checks itself (#432, #565, packs/basics/tasks/baselining/
+// worker.mjs `ciDispatchPlan`). A workflow that cannot be dispatched cannot be
+// started, so auto-merge waits on a check that will never exist.
+//
+// ClaudiniteWebsite sat in exactly this: an unfiltered ci.yml running the sweep
+// on `pull_request`, no `workflow_dispatch:`, and a maintenance PR that had to
+// be merged by hand. The worker already LOGS this case as `missing`, but a line
+// in a scheduler log nobody reads is not a gate — hence the rule.
+//
 // ADVISORY, DELIBERATELY. Shipping this blocking would turn every member that
 // lacks the workflow red on its very next baselining — which is precisely the
 // #555 failure this rule exists to make less likely (a required thing added to
@@ -69,31 +85,54 @@ export function gatesEveryPull(text) {
   };
 }

+// Can baselining START this workflow? The GITHUB_TOKEN recursion guard means a
+// bot-authored PR never gets a `pull_request` run, so `workflow_dispatch:` is
+// what makes the conformance gate reachable at all on the maintenance PR.
+export function isDispatchable(text) {
+  return /^\s+workflow_dispatch:/m.test(onBlock(text));
+}
+
 const rule = {
   id: &#39;conformance-workflow&#39;,
   severity: &#39;advisory&#39;,
-  description: &#39;A member has a workflow running check_the_world on every pull request, with no path filter&#39;,
+  description: &#39;A member has a workflow running check_the_world on every pull request — unfiltered, and dispatchable so baselining can start it&#39;,
   doc: &#39;packs/basics/scheduled-tasks.md&#39;,
-  why: &#39;auto-merge is a queue for checks — a path-filtered conformance flow arms successfully and then never runs, so the nightly maintenance PR waits forever and the repo silently stops baselining&#39;,
+  why: &#39;auto-merge is a queue for checks — a conformance flow that is path-filtered, or that baselining cannot dispatch, arms successfully and then never runs, so the nightly maintenance PR waits forever and the repo silently stops baselining&#39;,

   run(ctx) {
     if (!ctx.files.includes(MOUNT)) return [];  // not a member — inert
     const workflows = ctx.files.filter((f) =&gt; f.startsWith(WORKFLOW_DIR) &amp;&amp; /\.ya?ml$/.test(f));

     const filtered = [];
+    const undispatchable = [];
     for (const file of workflows) {
       const text = ctx.read(file);
       if (text === null) continue;
       const { pull, filtered: hasFilter, sweeps } = gatesEveryPull(text);
       if (!pull || !sweeps) continue;
-      if (!hasFilter) return [];              // a real gate exists — done
-      filtered.push(file);
+      if (hasFilter) { filtered.push(file); continue; }
+      // An unfiltered sweep — a real gate, but only if baselining can start it.
+      if (!isDispatchable(text)) { undispatchable.push(file); continue; }
+      return [];                              // a reachable gate exists — done
     }

     // A repo with NO pull_request workflow at all is not flagged: baselining
     // merges its maintenance PR directly (#588), which is a coherent shape. The
     // finding is for the trap — a sweep that exists but cannot run.
-    if (!filtered.length &amp;&amp; !workflows.some((f) =&gt; /^\s+pull_request:/m.test(ctx.read(f) ?? &#39;&#39;))) return [];
+    if (!filtered.length &amp;&amp; !undispatchable.length
+      &amp;&amp; !workflows.some((f) =&gt; /^\s+pull_request:/m.test(ctx.read(f) ?? &#39;&#39;))) return [];
